A||N||4||terryk||Rear deck lid adjustment?||||5||
Z||000000||terryk||01-02-2001||07:15 PM||||Has any figured out a systematic way of adjusting the rear lid? It's starting to feel rather random. <P>The front is too high and it appears the hinges are all of the way down. Strange. <P>I had to remove it to do the swap and it's just way too much fun to get it adjusted again.||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||01-02-2001||09:23 PM||||Try unloading the torsion bars. That will unload the hinge and should make it easier to set the height. Be real careful with the rods.<P>If you take the grates out and align it across the back you'll hopefully be able to tighten the lid to the hinge with it shut. <P>You can set the front edge centering easier by making some chalk marks before removing the grates. Even if you can only get one nut on each hinge this way it should be easier, especially if your by yourself.<P>Be carefull when you put the grates back to close it slowly the first time just in case it's a little off. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||01-02-2001||09:49 PM||||Ok, I'll go back through that. The front is clearly too high.<P>Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||01-03-2001||02:46 PM||||Got out the old straight-edge and the trunk lid is warped. The right side that goes along the back of the grill is 1" higher than the left. <P>I had a gas shock lift on there for a period of time (without the torsion springs). I think that contributed to the problem. It was fine before the gas lift.<P>I've got it under pressure to see if it will relax and come back down. Maybe yes, maybe no.<P>TK||||reg||11
Z||000004||theogre||01-03-2001||07:48 PM||||that bites. It might help if you get it good and warm then put a good load on it.||||reg||1
